{{Nihongo|Kazuki Hino|日野 一樹|Hino Kazuki|born May 15, 1980}} is a [[Sanese people|Sanese]] ([[Okaiken]]) professional [[w:bodybuilder|bodybuilder]], widely regarded as one of the greatest heavyweight competitors in the history of the sport. He is best known for his record-tying eight-year reign as '''[[Mr. Adonia]]''' (2007–2015), the most prestigious title in professional bodybuilding.

Nicknamed "'''The Iron Monk'''" and "'''The Saitama Beast'''", Hino is celebrated for his immense size, "grainy" conditioning, and brutal "high-intensity" training philosophy. He is credited with popularizing the "Mass Monster" era in Okaiken and establishing the country as a powerhouse in global bodybuilding. In 2015, he retired undefeated as the reigning champion.

==Early life==
Hino was born in [[Saitama]], [[Miyagi Prefecture]], [[Okaiken]], to a working-class family. In his youth, he was described as tall but exceptionally lean ("lanky"). He initially pursued [[volleyball]] at Saitama North High School but was cut from the team at age 15 due to a perceived lack of "physical presence." This rejection, often cited as his "origin story," drove him to seek a sport where physical mass was the primary metric of success.

In 1995, Hino discovered a magazine featuring [[Shayden|Shaydian]] bodybuilder [[Rick Dixon|Rick "The Mason" Dixon]]. Inspired by Dixon's dense, granite-like physique, Hino began training at a local municipal gym, adopting a "blood and guts" philosophy that emphasized heavy compound movements and extreme intensity. He taught himself English by watching Dixon's training VHS tapes, adopting the curse words as "mantras of power."

==Bodybuilding career==

===Amateur career===
Hino rose quickly through the amateur ranks in Okaiken, known for overwhelming competitors with sheer size. In 2005, he won the '''Okaiken Pro''', his first major national title, weighing in at {{convert|128|kg|lb|abbr=on}}. This victory qualified him for the international circuit. The victory was decisive; judges noted that his leg development was already years ahead of the domestic standard.

===International debut and early struggles (2006)===
Hino's international debut at the 2006 '''Shayden Grand Prix''' was a humbling experience. Despite his size, he placed 3rd, losing to more aesthetically polished competitors. Critics argued that while Hino possessed "freakish" mass, he lacked the stage presence and refined posing of the top-tier western pros. Hino took this criticism personally, retreating to his "dungeon" gym for a year of isolation training to refine his conditioning.

===The Adonia Dynasty (2007–2015)===
Hino returned in 2007 to the '''Mr. Adonia''' stage, the sport's highest honor. Weighing in at a shredded {{convert|135|kg|lb|abbr=on}}, he shocked the bodybuilding world by defeating the reigning champion in a landslide victory. His physique displayed a new "grainy" texture—a look of extreme muscle hardness and separation—that would become his signature.

Between 2008 and 2010, Hino established dominance, winning every title with perfect scores. This period is often referred to as "The Era of Granite." His routine of hitting a "Most Muscular" pose while screaming guttural curses became a fan-favorite spectacle, terrorizing opponents who attempted to out-size him.

The middle of his reign (2011–2012) was defined by his intense rivalry with Shayden bodybuilder '''Matt Mulhouse'''. Mulhouse represented the "aesthetic" antithesis to Hino's "mass monster" style. The rivalry reached its peak at the 2012 Mr. Adonia, where Hino allegedly engaged in a physical intimidation tactic backstage, slamming into Mulhouse before the final posedown. Hino won the title, cementing the dominance of the "mass" philosophy.

In 2013, Hino faced his toughest challenge after suffering a partial pec tear three weeks before the competition. Competing through injury, he relied on his superior leg development and back width to edge out a victory by a single point. He returned in 2014 fully healed and larger than ever, delivering what many historians consider the greatest package of his career.

Hino announced his retirement immediately after winning his eighth title in 2015, leaving his posing trunks on the center stage podium. He retired undefeated as Mr. Adonia, stating he wished to focus on raising his son, [[Renji Hino]].

